00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.200
Like we've made this podcast
about talking about regret.

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:34.750
Don't do that with people you talk to.

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:38.470
Don't engage in regret just
in casual conversation,

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:39.940
cause it's a downer.

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:42.603
We're professionals, we
know how to keep it up.

00:31:43.894 --> 00:31:46.970
But what if it's somebody
that you're trying to

00:31:46.970 --> 00:31:48.560
impress, or you think there might be

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:50.292
a future friendship or
relationship with them.

00:31:50.292 --> 00:31:51.800
If it's the first date

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:55.860
it can be like, yeah, I'm looking for

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:56.693
something to go over this,

00:31:56.693 --> 00:31:59.138
and I think it might be your face.

00:32:02.723 --> 00:32:04.650
I meant get a new tattoo,

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:07.630
I wasn't talking about the
below the waistband again.

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:08.463
Oh, right,

00:32:08.463 --> 00:32:10.870
cause you were gesturing to
your shoulder as you did it.

00:32:12.383 --> 00:32:14.110
And that is one strategy.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:15.820
What I was gonna say was,

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:18.753
I just think simply saying, you know what,

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:21.910
I thought it was funny at the time.

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:23.400
You see somebody make eye contact with it,

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.070
I thought it was funny at the time.

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:26.210
You know what, still pretty funny.

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:27.760
Well if you're gonna do that,

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:30.330
tattoo that underneath it.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:32.750
Okay, that's a good update.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:34.280
I thought this was funny at the time.

00:32:34.280 --> 00:32:37.330
I thought this was funny in 1998,

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:38.867
or whatever it was.

00:32:38.867 --> 00:32:41.640
And this was actually not that long ago

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:43.290
if it was wrecking ball,

00:32:43.290 --> 00:32:44.880
that's a few years, I don't know.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:46.210
I've lost track of time.

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:47.663
What about, we had a friend,

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.800
who, you know, there are
the kids in high school

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:53.560
that started getting
tattoos their senior year

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:54.680
when they turned 18, right?

00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:56.203
Mhm.
There's,

00:32:57.550 --> 00:33:00.036
especially back in 1996

00:33:00.036 --> 00:33:05.033
when we were moving into our
senior year of high school,

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.850
there were the kids that
turned 18 and got tattoos,

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:11.600
and it was like, oh, really,

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:13.610
I'm sure that's a lot more
common than it is now,

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:15.370
and also there's a lot of kids
who were younger than that

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:17.560
who go in with their parents
permission and get tattoos.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:18.850
You talking about Pearl Jam tattoo,

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:20.695
or are you talking about
Tasmanian devil tattoo?

00:33:20.695 --> 00:33:21.750
I'm talking about Tasmanian,

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:22.750
I'm thinking about both,

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:24.739
but I wanted to talk
about Tasmanian devil.

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:27.050
Because we had a friend who came--

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:28.753
Yeah.
To school one day,

00:33:31.106 --> 00:33:33.860
and he had gotten a full color,

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:36.980
like it looked like I had
just tuned into a cartoon,

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:37.813
you know what I'm saying?

00:33:37.813 --> 00:33:39.450
It was just, it was a freeze frame

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:40.823
from a Warner Brothers,

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:44.151
is that what the Tasmanian Devil's from?

00:33:44.151 --> 00:33:45.430
I don't know.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:50.346
It was the Tasmanian Devil
ripping out of his delt,

00:33:50.346 --> 00:33:52.080
and coming to say hello.

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:55.140
And, but in a spin,

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:57.010
he was coming out, he had broken through

00:33:57.010 --> 00:33:58.480
in the Tasmanian Devil spin.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:33:59.610
The tornado.
Yeah.

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:03.210
And even at that age, at that time

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:07.233
we felt bad for him.
Not a good look.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:09.610
It's tough.

00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:11.760
It's tough--
No way he's still got that.

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.610
It's tough to react to a tattoo.

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:15.500
No way he's still got that tattoo.

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:16.563
I bet he does.

00:34:17.870 --> 00:34:19.980
Do you think he's got it
covered up with other things?

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:21.920
Yeah, yeah, I bet he's got other things.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:23.610
Because the one you're
thinking about right now,

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:27.110
Pearl Jam, the Pearl Jam tattoo
was covered up by college.

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:27.950
Yep, that's true.

00:34:27.950 --> 00:34:30.240
But literally, like, two years later

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:32.690
the Pearl Jam tattoo was
completely covered over.

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:35.590
And that wasn't even that bad of a tattoo.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:36.560
No it wasn't.

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:38.100
And now I think it's come back.

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:38.933
You know, it's like,

00:34:38.933 --> 00:34:40.520
if you had it that long,

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:42.270
now's a good time to
have a Pearl Jam tattoo.

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:45.190
Can you get one layer of
a cover up tattoo removed

00:34:45.190 --> 00:34:46.750
to reveal a previous tattoo?

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:49.500
Like an archeological tattoo removal?

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:51.190
It's just like, do you want the tattoo

00:34:51.190 --> 00:34:53.470
that you used to regret?

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:55.150
Can Dr. Tat Off do that?

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:56.363
Uh uh, no.

00:34:57.287 --> 00:34:58.920
I don't, I mean, I don't
know how poison ivy works,

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:00.380
apparently, according to everybody.

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:01.520
It's very clear that you don't.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.225
But I do know how tat offs work.

00:35:04.225 --> 00:35:05.858
What about this?

00:35:05.858 --> 00:35:07.191
Mythical_alenna,

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:11.067
"Eating a soggy burrito.

00:35:11.067 --> 00:35:13.337
"I tried to heat it up
in a pan, but it stuck,

00:35:13.337 --> 00:35:15.253
"so I scraped it off, and ate it wet.

00:35:16.237 --> 00:35:20.380
"Ate a wet, cold burrito, that
was also hot in some spots."

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:21.213
Oh.

00:35:21.213 --> 00:35:23.068
"It had avocado and shrimp in it too."

00:35:23.068 --> 00:35:23.901
Whew!

00:35:23.901 --> 00:35:25.387
"I vomit just thinking about it."

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:28.437
That's very specific.

00:35:28.437 --> 00:35:29.760
That's a very specific regret.

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:32.233
Yeah, that's the, that is a specific one.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:36.540
I've never tried to heat
up a burrito like that.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.710
Whenever I have the
second half of a burrito,

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:39.660
I just eat it cold.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.949
Do you do this with other
foods, or just burritos?

00:35:41.949 --> 00:35:43.077
Most--

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:44.923
What about pizza?

00:35:44.923 --> 00:35:46.330
Cold.

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:47.990
Fried chicken, cold.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:50.831
Well, yeah, but both of those
things that you mentioned,

00:35:50.831 --> 00:35:52.503
fried pizza?

00:35:52.503 --> 00:35:55.520
Fried chicken and pizza kind of

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:58.122
have their own character
cold, but a burrito?

00:35:58.122 --> 00:35:58.955
Yeah.

00:35:58.955 --> 00:36:00.210
Doesn't have character cold.

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:02.650
Like a Chipotle burrito, they're so huge,

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:04.530
I'll cut them in half,
and then I'll, like,

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:05.750
I'll unwrap the foil,

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:07.220
and I'll just eat the second half.

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:08.260
It's difficult to heat up,

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:09.503
I will give you that much.

00:36:09.503 --> 00:36:11.590
It's not that good.

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:13.313
One of the things that I regret.

00:36:14.735 --> 00:36:17.970
I don't know why this pops into my head,

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:20.573
but it's a bit sad, but bear with me.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:23.360
Remember when we went
on that off-roading trip

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:25.663
with the group of people
that we didn't know?

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:29.177
And the one guy was the leader,
the organizer of the trip,

00:36:29.177 --> 00:36:32.550
and it's like a four day
trip in Death Valley,

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:33.920
it was the first time we went.

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.160
He was the leader,

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:38.410
and then he, this tragic
thing happened to him

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:41.770
when, like, his child passed away.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:42.780
Right.

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:44.060
His young child.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:45.960
And he lived a long ways a way,

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:47.640
he like lived in Northern California.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:48.490
Yeah.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:50.530
But our friend Nick knew him better

00:36:50.530 --> 00:36:52.400
because, like, he had connected with him,

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:54.710
met him, and that's how we got,

00:36:54.710 --> 00:36:57.710
that's how he got invited to the trip,

00:36:57.710 --> 00:36:58.660
and then we got invited,

00:36:58.660 --> 00:36:59.493
we got to know him.

00:36:59.493 --> 00:37:01.470
So, Nick knew him better.

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:02.303
But,

00:37:04.163 --> 00:37:09.163
I had an opportunity to
go with Nick up to the,

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:11.740
like, the funeral service,

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:13.750
and I just, for some reason,

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.423
I really regret not going.

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:19.582
Like, I just feel like,

00:37:19.582 --> 00:37:20.920
I feel like it was one of
those things where it's like

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:22.390
that was the right thing to do,

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:24.890
and yeah, I had my rationale, I was like,

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:26.340
we would have had to, it was so far

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:29.189
that we'd have to stay overnight, or like,

00:37:29.189 --> 00:37:30.820
it was a long freaking drive

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:32.110
to get to where this thing was,

00:37:32.110 --> 00:37:33.220
and then it's like, I,

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:36.553
you know, I only knew
him at this one trip,

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:41.870
you know, we're not actually friends.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:43.220
It was very easy to say, well,

00:37:43.220 --> 00:37:44.260
it's not my place to go there,

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:46.893
but then it kinds of eats
away at me, even now,

00:37:46.893 --> 00:37:49.230
that like, I didn't go,

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:51.540
cause I felt like, you know,

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:53.953
when somebody goes through
something that horrible,

00:37:54.994 --> 00:37:56.080
it's just like, wow,

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:58.030
people I didn't expect
came out of the woodwork

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:00.799
just to show up, and I
know it was along drive.

00:38:00.799 --> 00:38:03.630
I think it would've meant a lot to him

00:38:03.630 --> 00:38:05.290
that somebody who barely knew him

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:06.690
took the time to go up there.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:09.130
And I think Nick went anyway.

00:38:09.130 --> 00:38:11.163
So many I think too highly of myself.

00:38:12.526 --> 00:38:14.490
But it's weird that I
fixate on that, right?

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:16.270
Yeah, cause listen, I'm not,

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:18.810
first of all, I think
in some circumstances

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:20.730
you kind of got, you gotta
let yourself off the hook.

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:25.050
Listen, because in my mind the moment

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:28.210
that I begin thinking about
all the things that I could do,

00:38:28.210 --> 00:38:29.650
all the choices that I could make

00:38:29.650 --> 00:38:31.764
that would be selfless or whatever,

00:38:31.764 --> 00:38:34.892
I'm gonna have all
kinds of regrets, right?

00:38:34.892 --> 00:38:37.843
In that particular situation,

00:38:37.843 --> 00:38:40.810
obviously I felt horrible
about what was going on,

00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:45.730
but we literally, like, Nick
had been on multiple trips

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:48.056
with him, and like talked
to him on the phone.

00:38:48.056 --> 00:38:48.889
Yeah.

00:38:48.889 --> 00:38:50.197
Was a friend, like.
Yeah.

00:38:50.197 --> 00:38:51.228
They were friends.

00:38:51.228 --> 00:38:53.050
We went on a trip that
had four or five cars

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.340
in a caravan, and like,
there was a couple of times

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:56.490
where we had a conversation

00:38:56.490 --> 00:38:58.060
while we're sitting around a fire.

00:38:58.060 --> 00:38:59.968
He would probably have a hard
time remembering our names.

00:38:59.968 --> 00:39:03.370
It would've, in my mind it would've been

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:06.033
a little bit strange.
Okay.

00:39:06.033 --> 00:39:07.041
For you to go.

00:39:07.041 --> 00:39:09.113
That's my take on it.

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:14.000
So I definitely don't think
you should feel bad about it.

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:17.140
Yeah, I guess I just feel so bad for him.

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:18.680
I completely get that.

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:20.100
Yeah, so that I would,

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:24.270
it's like well, that's
why it stays in my brain

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:26.850
is cause it was such a tragic thing

00:39:26.850 --> 00:39:29.420
that like, I didn't, you know,

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:30.930
I actually didn't do anything.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.769
It moved me, but

00:39:32.769 --> 00:39:37.097
it didn't actually move
me transportationally.

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:39.379
I get it.

00:39:39.379 --> 00:39:40.757
I think that's why it stuck in my brain.

00:39:40.757 --> 00:39:42.730
And I'm sure some people would interpret

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:43.750
what I'm saying as cold hearted,

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:45.070
but I'm saying that I think,

00:39:45.070 --> 00:39:49.720
and it's not, like I have every,

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:51.320
I have sympathy.
Yeah sure.

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:52.793
For the guy, but,

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:58.530
I don't know, I feel like
there's lots of situations,

00:39:58.530 --> 00:40:00.600
cause when you think about it, you know.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:01.433
Well, here's another one--

00:40:01.433 --> 00:40:02.630
Well think about this,

00:40:02.630 --> 00:40:05.809
I mean, Jessie and I talk
about this, you know,

00:40:05.809 --> 00:40:09.887
we try to be pretty charitable, right?

00:40:09.887 --> 00:40:13.150
There's plenty of things
that we can give to,

00:40:13.150 --> 00:40:15.800
and we try to be strategic,

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:18.683
and sort of effective in
the things that we give to.

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:22.520
But then we also do
things like spend money

00:40:22.520 --> 00:40:25.788
on making our yard nice, right?

00:40:25.788 --> 00:40:29.770
And there's a, and I'm less,

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:34.140
like if I were to, like,
present it to Jessie,

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:35.380
and I were to say, you know what,

00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:37.090
we could make the pool nicer,

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:38.270
or we could take all that money

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:40.983
and we could give it to
somebody who needs it, right?

00:40:40.983 --> 00:40:42.270
Mhm.

00:40:42.270 --> 00:40:43.666
And it's just like, okay,

00:40:43.666 --> 00:40:47.100
from a strict moral standpoint,

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:52.100
should you give away all
your money, and everything,

00:40:53.130 --> 00:40:55.750
and do nothing for yourself
until you have done

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:57.450
all those things for other people?

00:40:58.590 --> 00:41:01.295
It's like, I get into
a place where I'm like,

00:41:01.295 --> 00:41:03.350
I mean, I'm sure there's somebody

00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:05.240
who's gonna live their life like that,

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:07.890
but I'm like, okay, I don't
wanna be irresponsible,

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:09.870
and I wanna be generous,

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:12.240
but I also wanna spend some money

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:13.540
on something that I feel like

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.860
me and my family are gonna enjoy.

00:41:15.860 --> 00:41:17.620
But I can't sit around and think, like,

00:41:17.620 --> 00:41:20.200
oh, you know what, if you
hadn't of gotten that,

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:22.460
if you hadn't of installed this thing

00:41:22.460 --> 00:41:23.610
at your house that you wanted,

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:25.610
and put these speakers
up in your backyard,

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:27.980
you could have given
speakers to somebody else.

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:29.090
Yeah.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:30.190
I just don't think that's a,

00:41:30.190 --> 00:41:32.773
I just don't it's a
healthy way to live because

00:41:32.773 --> 00:41:37.113
you can always find something that you

00:41:38.096 --> 00:41:40.633
can make yourself feel obligated to do.

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:45.163
Cause it also, in another
sense it also makes you,

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:48.990
it centers you.
Yeah.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:50.470
In the conversation.

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:53.270
Like, oh, well if I had of done this,

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:54.830
it would've solved the problem,

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:56.700
or it would have meant
that much to this guy

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:57.660
if I had of done that.

00:41:57.660 --> 00:41:58.985
Yeah.

00:41:58.985 --> 00:42:00.321
There is an element of it

00:42:00.321 --> 00:42:01.588
that gets into centering yourself.

00:42:01.588 --> 00:42:02.421
I think in that it's like,

00:42:02.421 --> 00:42:04.130
I met the guy once, I don't know him,

00:42:04.130 --> 00:42:06.710
I'm not really his friend, it's like,

00:42:06.710 --> 00:42:08.810
and I think when Nick asked me to go,

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:11.070
you had something planned specific,

00:42:11.070 --> 00:42:13.570
but I didn't have anything
specifically planned,

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:16.170
and so Nick asked, "Do
you wanna go with me?"

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:17.780
So he didn't have to drive
